<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
   The default user, and why this file has to exist
  ============================================================================
   The image's entrypoint checks for CLICKHOUSE_USER and CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD.
   Finding neither, it writes /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/default-user.xml
   restricting the `default` user to 127.0.0.1 and ::1, and says so in one log
   line at startup:

       /entrypoint.sh: neither CLICKHOUSE_USER nor CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD is set,
       disabling network access for user 'default'

   That is a sensible default for a single server exposed to the world. On a
   cluster it breaks everything that crosses a node boundary:

       DB::Exception: Received from clickhouse-02:9000.
       default: Authentication failed: password is incorrect, or there is no
       user with such name. (AUTHENTICATION_FAILED)

   The confusing part is what still works. `CREATE ... ON CLUSTER` succeeds,
   because it is not a connection between nodes at all: the statement goes
   into a queue in Keeper and each node picks it up and runs it locally. So
   the cluster looks configured, DDL propagates, and only a genuinely
   distributed query — clusterAllReplicas, a Distributed table, a replica
   fetching a part — fails. One broken thing hidden behind a working one.

   This file restores network access. It is named 99- so that it merges after
   the entrypoint's default-user.xml and wins.

   No password, deliberately. Adding one here would mean also putting
   credentials into remote_servers for distributed queries and into
   interserver_http_credentials for replication — three places to keep in
   step, for a cluster whose ports are not published outside this machine.
   Revisit before anything here is reachable from elsewhere.
  ============================================================================
-->
<clickhouse>
    <users>
        <default>
            <!-- Empty rather than absent: an absent password element would
                 leave whatever the merged configuration had before. -->
            <password></password>

            <!-- Any address. In practice that means the dataplatform docker
                 network, since only node 1 publishes a native port and only
                 to localhost. -->
            <networks replace="replace">
                <ip>::/0</ip>
            </networks>

            <profile>default</profile>
            <quota>default</quota>

            <!-- Needed for CREATE ... ON CLUSTER and for managing users later.
                 Without it the default user can query but not grant. -->
            <access_management>1</access_management>
        </default>
    </users>
</clickhouse>
